On Apr 29, 7:01=A0pm, Jove <inva...@invalid.invalid> wrote:
>
> =A0 You're right. =A0The #enhance screen is an out-of-game redundant
> player decision that can only reflect decisions the player has already
> made in-game. =A0It's also redundant in that roles already have limited
> skill levels. =A0If player characteristics like Str, Dx, Co, In, etc.
> can be increased/decreased irrespective of XL there's no reason weapon
> and spell skills can't.

Then the player should start with the full complement of
29 slots ready and available at XL=3D1.  Since the addition
of exercising and abusing intrinsics to increase/decrease
them it has made sense that being able to take specific
actions could increase ability.  The skill system has
improved on that model.

> =A0 There's no need for weapon/spell skills to decay.

Unless you want the equivalent of abusing an intrinsic.
The difference - Abusing wisdom takes specific actions
but this decay takes inaction.  Figure out a way to abuse
a spell/weapon skill and I'll view the suggestion differently.

I object to automating the skills system since skill slots
are a limited resource.  Playing a wizard I've enhanced an
assortment of weapon classes before getting armor that
does not interfere with spell casting and I ended up needing
to be extremely carefully about enhancing spell schools.
I've played samurai who enhanced enough weapons classes
that I couldn't max out both dagger and bow and ended up
with samurai skewed toward extra melee rather than a
blend of ranger plus knight.  But I don't object to starting
with the full pool of slots and if an abuse system could be
devised that would handle the limited nature of slots.

> Only one, or at
> most two, weapons can be used at one time. =A0Carrying around every
> weapon the character can increase skill in would be its own punishment
> in terms of weight. =A0Spells are limited by mana, which is itself
> already mostly dependent on XL. =A0Spell failure rates are also already
> affected by XL. =A0Again, skill slots based on XL are redundant.

Keeping a weapon for a skill slot in the inventory is one
way to handle deterioration for weapons skills.  No longer
want expert in long sword?  Stop carrying any katana or
long sword in open inventory or the bag.  I think that would
work as an abuse method.  But I can't think of a way to do
that with spell skills - In a speed game there wouldn't be
time to use forgetting spells as an abuse while in an
endurance game there is plenty of time when spells are
forgotten and the books for the school are in a stash.

> =A0 So automatically #enhance skills when they've been exercised enough.
> just keep the #enhance screen so the player can check what skills are
> possible for the role and what level has been achieved.

Or let the #enhance pick a skill to be decreased.  I don't
like that either because it lets a player pick and chose over
a wider range.


